then to display the information about the commit we'll use a jj log template that outputs the information about the commit in json and use that to echo the information about each commit, using json keys

you can run it from

the output from the above jj template will have some graph nodes and/or edges, then some whitespace before the JSON payload

we'll create a templating DSL

newlines in this string will be spaces, and the character literal "\n" in the string will mean a newline - so the template a few lines below would be a single line of output

anything in %{} is either a json key, or a "function" call in our DSL, see functions below

'''
%{elastic_tab(change_id)}
%{elastic_tab(commit_id)}
%{elastic_tab(author)}
%{elastic_tab(timestamp)}
%{working_copies}
%{bookmarks}
%{tags}
%{description}
'''

elastic_tab(...) aligns the left side of its context as per current columnar alignment behaviour for eg. the change id. Any space greater than 1 will use the current behaviour of dashes including the dash start and end chars


